survived.evidence:
Probe on the PR's sweep(). With MOD(`class P {
limit = 3
run(cmd, args, t) { return spawnSync(cmd, args, { timeout: t }); }
go() { return this.run("gh", ["x"], Number(process.env.X)); }
}`) the output was {"spawns":[],"problems":[]}. With `limit = 3;` the output was {"spawns":[{"where":"x.mjs:5","bounded":true,"reaches":["reads process.env.X"],"range":["takes a timeout that does not work out to a number: t"]}],"problems":[]}. In membersOf, `head` is reset only on `;` or after a `{…}` block, so the line after an ASI-terminated field is not read as a member head. This is not a regression, since the old sweep was blind to the whole class, but it is a gap in the class reading the PR introduces. No linter in the repo enforces semicolons.

unverified.evidence:
Probe on the PR's sweep(): MOD(`class P {
run(cmd, args) { return spawnSync(cmd, args, {timeout: 5000}); }
go() { return [1].map(function () { return this.run("gh", ["x"]); }); }
}`) gave spawns [{"where":"x.mjs:4","bounded":true,…}]. The sweep treats the call as bounded through P.run, while at runtime `this` is undefined or whatever `.call()` supplies. I did not build a case where this hides an unbounded spawn on its own; that would need another class's method funnel, which is the first finding.
